From: Omarchy Mobile <omamobile@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] i2c: qcom-cci: don't turn the clocks on at resume for a suspended device

cci_suspend() does nothing when the device is already runtime suspended,
but cci_resume() enables the clocks unconditionally and then asks for an
autosuspend. The device's runtime PM status is still "suspended", so the
autosuspend request does nothing and the clocks stay on: each system
resume of an idle CCI leaks one enable of every CCI clock.

On a Redmi 4 Prime (msm8953) the four CCI clocks read an enable count of
6 after six s2idle cycles while the CCI was runtime suspended. They are
fed from the RPM XO clock, so the apps side kept its XO vote in the RPM
sleep set and the SoC never reached XO shutdown.

Use pm_runtime_force_suspend() and pm_runtime_force_resume(), which only
resume the device at system resume when it was active before the
suspend.

---
 drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qcom-cci.c | 18 +-----------------
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 17 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qcom-cci.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qcom-cci.c
@@ -493,24 +493,8 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
-static int __maybe_unused cci_suspend(struct device *dev)
-{
-	if (!pm_runtime_suspended(dev))
-		return cci_suspend_runtime(dev);
-
-	return 0;
-}
-
-static int __maybe_unused cci_resume(struct device *dev)
-{
-	cci_resume_runtime(dev);
-	pm_request_autosuspend(dev);
-
-	return 0;
-}
-
 static const struct dev_pm_ops qcom_cci_pm = {
-	SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(cci_suspend, cci_resume)
+	SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(pm_runtime_force_suspend, pm_runtime_force_resume)
 	SET_RUNTIME_PM_OPS(cci_suspend_runtime, cci_resume_runtime, NULL)
 };
 
